# Day 4 – Patent Citation Analysis using Recursive Queries in PostgreSQL

## Objective

The objective of this exercise is to model patent citation relationships and analyze them using PostgreSQL recursive queries.


--

# Source Table

```
citation.patent_training
```

Contains

- publication_number
- publication_date
- title
- abstract
- inventor_name
- other patent information

---

# Task 1 – Database Design

## Objective

Create a table representing citation relationships between patents.

Each row represents

```
Patent A  ----->  Patent B
(citing)         (cited)
```

where Patent A references Patent B.

---

## Table Structure

```
citation.patent_citations
```

Columns

| Column | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| citing_publication | Patent making the citation |
| cited_publication | Referenced older patent |

Primary Key

```
(citing_publication,
 cited_publication)
```

Foreign Keys

```
citation.patent_training(publication_number)
```

for both columns.

---
```sql
CREATE TABLE citation.patent_citations
(
    citing_publication TEXT NOT NULL,
    cited_publication  TEXT NOT NULL,

    CONSTRAINT pk_patent_citations
        PRIMARY KEY (citing_publication, cited_publication),

    CONSTRAINT fk_citing_publication
        FOREIGN KEY (citing_publication)
        REFERENCES citation.patent_training (publication_number),

    CONSTRAINT fk_cited_publication
        FOREIGN KEY (cited_publication)
        REFERENCES citation.patent_training (publication_number)
);
```

## Why?

A patent can

- cite many patents
- be cited by many patents

This represents a many-to-many relationship.

---

# Task 2 – Test Data Generation

## Objective

Generate realistic citation relationships.

Rules

- Every patent cites older patents only.
- Each patent cites between 1 and 4 patents.
- Duplicate citations are prevented.
- Self-citations are not allowed.

---

## Approach

Assign every patent a sequence number ordered by

```
publication_date
publication_number
```

For every patent

```
Randomly choose older sequence numbers
```

and insert citation records.

---

## Result

Example

| Patent | Cites |
|---------|-------|
| US1009 | US1003 |
| US1009 | US1005 |
| US1015 | US1002 |
| US1015 | US1007 |

---

# Task 3 – Recursive Query

## Objective

Retrieve the complete citation hierarchy.

Input

```
Publication Number
```

Output

```
Patent
 ├── Direct Citation
 │     ├── Older Citation
 │     │      ├── Older Citation
 │     │      │      ...
```

---

## Technique Used

```
WITH RECURSIVE
```

Recursive CTE repeatedly joins the citation table until no more older patents exist.

---

## Output Columns

| Column | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Root Patent | Original publication |
| Cited Patent | Patent found in hierarchy |
| Depth | Level of citation |

Example

| Root | Citation | Depth |
|------|----------|------|
| US1009 | US1005 | 1 |
| US1009 | US0998 | 2 |
| US1009 | US0981 | 3 |

---

# Task 4 – Database Function

## Objective

Encapsulate the recursive query inside a reusable PostgreSQL function.

Input

```
Publication Number
```

Returns

```
Entire citation hierarchy
```

---

## Benefits

Instead of writing recursive SQL repeatedly

```
SELECT *
FROM get_patent_citations('US123456');
```

can be executed.

Advantages

- reusable
- easier maintenance
- cleaner SQL
- reusable by reports and applications

---

# Task 5 – Function Usage

## Objective

Retrieve citation hierarchy for multiple patents.

Example

```
SELECT
publication_number,
function_result.*
FROM citation.patent_training
CROSS JOIN LATERAL
get_patent_citations(publication_number)
LIMIT 100;
```

---

## Benefit

The function executes once for each patent and can be combined with any query.

